1
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:18,233
SecureMailScope analyses email traffic captures. It is passive by design: no
host in a capture is ever contacted, and nothing leaves this machine.

2
00:00:18,233 --> 00:00:24,471
Nine synthetic captures go in, covering SMTP, IMAP and POP3.

3
00:00:24,471 --> 00:00:29,238
Analysis runs entirely locally, against the bytes in the capture.

4
00:00:29,238 --> 00:00:39,338
The investigation opens on its conclusion: seven high-priority cryptographic
issues require attention, and the weakest capture scores fifty-nine out of a
hundred.

5
00:00:39,338 --> 00:00:46,505
Beneath it: the TLS version of every session, the severity distribution, and
what the certificates showed.

6
00:00:46,505 --> 00:00:54,938
Then the findings themselves, beside the evidence timeline they were drawn
from.

7
00:00:54,938 --> 00:01:04,872
Each finding names the rule it failed, the session it came from and the
confidence the engine assigned.

8
00:01:09,359 --> 00:01:17,859
Static RSA key exchange. No forward secrecy: anyone who later obtains the
server's long-term key can decrypt this session.

9
00:01:17,859 --> 00:01:34,825
Packets 4 and 5 — the exact packets the rule was evaluated against. Open the
same two in Wireshark and check.

10
00:01:34,825 --> 00:01:42,459
A session reads as the chain it is: entry point, version, cipher suite, key
exchange, certificate.

11
00:01:42,459 --> 00:01:54,125
A TLS 1.3 certificate is encrypted on the wire. It is reported as not
available, with the reason — never guessed.

12
00:01:54,125 --> 00:02:01,392
A fingerprint groups configurations. It identifies a setup, not an operator
— and the page says so.

13
00:02:01,392 --> 00:02:12,759
Drift compares one endpoint across captures; where the clients asked
different questions it says inconclusive. Correlations and blast radius stay
inside the captures analysed.

14
00:02:12,759 --> 00:02:22,825
Every timeline event carries its evidence status: observed in a packet, or
inferred.

15
00:02:22,825 --> 00:02:32,192
The anomaly detector actually in use is a deterministic rarity baseline, not
a machine-learning model, and the interface says so.

16
00:02:32,192 --> 00:02:40,525
One canonical model produces JSON, a self-contained HTML document and a PDF.
Their facts cannot disagree.

17
00:02:40,525 --> 00:02:49,492
Here is that export, opened straight from disk. It fetches nothing external,
and it is the same report the engine just produced.

18
00:02:52,331 --> 00:03:01,331
And the same report as a PDF, paginated, with the generation metadata on
every page.
